    "evidence_text": "Soluble CCL21 functionally resembles the second CCR7 ligand, CCL19, which lacks anchoring residues and forms soluble gradients.\n\nCLEC-2 deficiency in DCs impaired their entry into lymphatics and trafficking to and within lymph nodes, thereby reducing T cell priming.\n\nWe now report that B cell sequestration requires lineage-specific regulation of migratory receptors by the transcription factor Klf2.\n\nThis work demonstrates that in wild-type animals, naive FO B cells are actively removed from the MZ, thus restricting their capacity to respond to blood-borne pathogens.\n\nMigratory dendritic cells transfer antigen to a lymph node-resident dendritic cell population for efficient CTL priming.\n\nThese results support the argument for initial transport of antigen by migrating DCs, followed by its transfer to the lymphoid-resident DCs for presentation and CTL priming.\n\nThe chemokine, CXCL13, plays a central role in guiding B cells to follicles whereas T-zone chemokines guide activated B cells to the T zone.\n\nDendritic cells, T cells and lymphatics: dialogues in migration and beyond.\n\nLeuyte trafficking into and within lymph vessels is mediated by an interply with lymphatic endothelial cells (LECs).",
